# Motion

Use motion to explain a change in state or position. Keep frequent interactions fast, animate only the properties that change, and preserve the result when motion is disabled.

## Example

The panel combines a fast icon response with a moderate content reveal. Reduced motion removes the movement while keeping the state change visible.

<DocsExample title="State transition" source={motionExampleSource}>
	<MotionExample />
</DocsExample>

## Durations

Choose duration by distance and frequency. Small or repeated feedback should finish sooner than a large surface transition.

<DocsCopyTable columns={durationColumns} rows={durationRows} copyLabel="Duration token copied" />

## Selection guide

<DocsTable columns={selectionColumns} rows={selectionRows} minWidth="var(--ds-sizing-4xl)" />

Exits are usually faster than entrances. High-frequency keyboard actions may skip motion.

## Usage

Name exact transition properties. Prefer `transform` and `opacity` for movement:

```css
.popover {
	opacity: 1;
	transform: scale(1);
	transition:
		opacity var(--ds-motion-duration-moderate) var(--ds-motion-easing-out),
		transform var(--ds-motion-duration-moderate) var(--ds-motion-easing-out);
}

.popover[data-state='closed'] {
	opacity: 0;
	transform: scale(0.97);
	transition-duration: var(--ds-motion-duration-fast);
}
```

For reduced motion, remove positional movement and retain brief opacity or color feedback:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
	.popover {
		transform: none;
		transition-duration: var(--ds-motion-duration-instant);
	}
}
```
